    {
      "source": "Doctors of Running",
      "priority": 3,
      "url": "https://www.doctorsofrunning.com/2025/04/nike-vaporfly-4-review-2025-midfoot-and.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The ZoomX midsole is generally firmer than previous Vaporfly versions, with a touch-firm heel that becomes more resilient after break-in and a firmer forefoot feel.",
        "Responsiveness: The carbon Flyplate, rocker geometry, and very low weight create a notably snappy, fast ride with quick toe-offs and better response at higher paces.",
        "Stability: The platform is stiff/non-flexible with a mild-to-moderately stiff heel counter, but mild heel slippage was noted and often needed tighter lacing for secure lockdown.",
        "Durability: Early outsole wear was observed and flagged as a potential longevity issue, with overall durability expectation described as average."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Road Trail Run",
      "priority": 4,
      "url": "https://www.roadtrailrun.com/2025/04/nike-vaporfly-4-multi-tester-review.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The VF4 has lower stack and about 5 mm less heel foam, giving more ground feel and a less cushioned heel/impact-protective ride than prior versions, with the plate feeling closer to the heel.",
        "Responsiveness: Testers consistently call out excellent energy return with quick landing-to-toe-off transitions and smooth turnover at both slower and faster paces, though a few missed the more aggressive rocker feel of earlier models.",
        "Stability: Foot hold is generally secure (narrower fit, supportive heel construction), but reduced midfoot width and rear geometry made the shoe feel wobblier for some in the rear/midfoot, and several noted heel lockdown could be better.",
        "Durability: Key concerns are outsole longevity, especially thinner heel rubber and reports of faster forefoot rubber wear versus older Vaporfly versions; upper materials seem improved in structure versus V3 but still need some break-in."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Believe in the Run",
      "priority": 5,
      "url": "https://believeintherun.com/shoe-reviews/nike-vaporfly-4-review/",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: Stack was cut to 36/30 mm (6 mm drop), so underfoot depth is reduced versus prior Vaporfly; heel sidewall bulges are intended to add back initial heel cushioning and impact protection.",
        "Responsiveness: ZoomX foam with the full-length carbon Flyplate is described as bouncy and responsive with strong pop; it feels smoother than Alphafly and slightly more aggressive/snappy than Vaporfly 3, especially at faster paces.",
        "Stability: The shoe is explicitly called unstable, with reported heel lift/slip; the very light race-focused build offers limited heel/lateral support and no strong indication of high torsional rigidity.",
        "Durability: Outsole moved to a Vaporfly 2-style layout with more full-coverage forefoot rubber, which should improve forefoot wear; long-term outsole/material longevity is not fully established in this review."
      ]
    }
  ],
